## Break-glass fallback
- Prompt **v2** is pre-registered and labelled **`development`** — **Run eval · development**
  jumps straight to the green run with no UI step at all.
- If a run errors, the page says so in scene (a service-notice card with one technical line) —
  it never drops a raw error in front of the room. Press the button again.
- A fresh seed sets **`production`→v{{ prompt_versions.v1 }}** and **`development`→v{{ prompt_versions.v2 }}**
  automatically, and re-seeding re-asserts them — so the red → green flip is always reproducible.
- Controls distinguish policy correctness from blanket approval: e.g. control
  `{{ control_example.trace_id }}` ({{ control_example.vehicle_type }} €{{ '{:,}'.format(control_example.list_price_eur) }})
  stays correctly **{{ control_example.expected_v2_decision }}** under v2.

## Developer mode — the same beats from a shell
> Not part of the presented demo. The runbook above is the delivered path: the kit ships as a
> cartridge and everything happens in the browser. These commands are for working **on** the
> kit — a local checkout, CI, or debugging a deployment.

```bash
synth experiment --config config/demo.yaml                       # production (v{{ prompt_versions.v1 }}, stale) → RED
synth experiment --config config/demo.yaml --label development   # development (v{{ prompt_versions.v2 }}, the fix) → GREEN
synth experiment --config config/demo.yaml --gate 0.95           # offline PASS-rate gate, no model calls
synth playground --config config/demo.yaml                       # serve the playground locally on :8000
```
These are the *same* runs the buttons trigger — identical dataset, identical task, identical
Dataset Run names — so a developer and a presenter land in the same place in Langfuse.

## Reset
Teardown is project-level (data is append-only within a 30-day merge window): spin up a **fresh project**, set its
keys in `.env`, and re-run `synth seed` — the seed is deterministic, so the project regenerates byte-for-byte.
